tk: DEEP-DIVE DOSSIER I · ✥
big: Genesis
lede: Everything the Empire claims about men, women, land and order is traced back to fifty chapters that spend most of their length undermining that claim.
calls:
k: Why this one is first
It's the source of the Empire's founding doctrine and of your own earliest religious education. Both need digging out, and this dossier is the model for the three that follow.
||
k: How to work it
Reference is about a third of these pages, and the rest is yours. A half-filled dossier is more useful than a blank one.
/end

@page rh="GENESIS · 1 OF 16 · THE BOOK ON THE RECORD" m="GEN 1.1; GEN 1–11; GEN 12–50; GEN 50.26; WESTERMANN, GENESIS 1–11; ENUMA ELISH · ATRAHASIS; TKC: TL"
# What it is
rec:
Name | Greek *genesis*, "origin." Hebrew *Bereshit*, "in the beginning." Hebrew books are named for their first words more than their contents.
Place | The first book of the Torah in every collection. Nobody disputes its position, and nearly everything else about it is discussed.
Authorship | Traditionally Moses. CONTESTED: Most critical scholars read it as woven from more than one source and period, with major editing in or after the exile, and the two creation accounts are the clearest seam. The classic documentary hypothesis has been heavily revised since the 1970s, and traditional readers hold to a single author.
Genres inside | Cosmology, genealogy, origin stories, family saga, legal precedent, poetry, dream reports and a novella-length court story in the Joseph chapters. Don't run one test across all fifty chapters.
Structure | Chapters 1–11, the primeval history, from creation to the scattering of peoples. Chapters 12–50, one family across four generations, ending with Joseph's coffin in Egypt (50.26). The seam shows, and the two halves behave differently.
Setting | No king, no priesthood and no temple. Everything happens in households, and households decide who inherits.
Neighbors | Mesopotamian creation and flood literature covers similar ground with different theology. Comparison is where Genesis's own claims become visible.
box: What the book is actually about, if it's about one thing
INTERPRETATION: Blessing that keeps passing to the wrong person. Younger over elder, the barren over the fertile, the deceiver over the dutiful, the foreigner into the family. A book used to justify hierarchy spends fifty chapters disrupting the hierarchy it names most often, birth order.
/end
prompt: What I already think this book says, before reading it again | fill

@page rh="GENESIS · 2 OF 16 · THE TWO BEGINNINGS" m="GEN 1.1–2.3; GEN 2.4–25; EXOD 18.4; DEUT 33.29; PS 121.1–2; EXOD 26.20; TRIBLE, GOD AND THE RHETORIC OF SEXUALITY"
# Two accounts, unreconciled
The book opens with two creation accounts in a row, differing in order, method, vocabulary and picture of God. The editors who assembled it kept both. It's the first thing Genesis teaches about how scripture works.
tabrl:
| | First account · 1.1–2.3 | Second account · 2.4b–25 |
| Name for God | Elohim, "God" | YHWH Elohim, "the LORD God" |
| Starting point | Dark water, the deep | Dry ground, no plants, no rain, no one to work it |
| Order | Light, sky, land and plants, lights, sea creatures and birds, land animals, humans last | The human first, then a garden, trees, animals, then the woman |
| Method | Speech and separation | Shaping dust, breathing, planting, building |
| Humans | Male and female together, both in God's image (1.27) | The human (*ha'adam*) from the ground (*ha'adamah*), the woman built from his side |
| Their task | Fill the earth, subdue it, have dominion over the creatures (1.28) | Work the garden and keep it (2.15) |
| Ends with | God resting on the seventh day | Two people naked and unashamed |
k: The helper · 2.18
God makes "a helper fit for him," *ʿezer kenegdo*. The noun *ʿezer* appears about twenty times in the Hebrew Bible, and most of them describe God helping Israel. It names whoever supplies what the other can't, so "assistant" is a poor gloss. *Kenegdo* means "corresponding to him." The word usually translated "rib," *tsela*, means "side" nearly everywhere else, including the sides of the tabernacle.
empire: What Dominion does with the pair
POSSIBLE: Teaches the second account as the operative one and the first as poetry. Order of creation becomes order of authority, "helper" becomes a job description, and dominion over the earth quietly becomes dominion inside the household. Not a word has to change. Only the choice of which account is doctrine.
/end
prompt: Which account I was taught, and which one I'd never noticed was separate | fill

@page rh="GENESIS · 3 OF 16 · THE IMAGE AND THE ORDER · WRITING" m="WRITING; GEN 1.26–28; GEN 2.15; VON RAD, GENESIS; TKC: DOM"
# Work the doctrine
Two doctrines come out of these chapters and both hold weight for the Empire. Write them out in Dominion's voice before you write your objection. The Empire's version has to be truly persuasive or Eden's crisis isn't a crisis.
prompt: 1 · The image of God. What the Empire says it means, and who it quietly leaves out | fill
prompt: 2 · Dominion as stewardship or as ownership. The Empire's argument at its best | fill
prompt: 3 · My objection, in one paragraph I'd defend out loud | fill
prompt: 4 · Where this lands in the manuscript: scene, chapter or catechism | fill
