@page rh="GENESIS · 4 OF 16 · CHAPTERS 1–11" m="GEN 3; GEN 4.7; GEN 4.8–17; GEN 6–9; GEN 11.1–9; SONG 7.10; 1 TIM 2.13–14; GILGAMESH XI · ATRAHASIS"
# The primeval history
INTERPRETATION: Four movements, each ending worse than it began, and each answered with a promise more than a punishment. The pattern is the argument.
rec:
The garden · 3 | The serpent asks a question and the woman answers, adding that they mustn't even touch the tree. She eats and gives fruit to her husband, "who was with her" (3.6). Only two things are cursed, the serpent and the ground. The woman isn't cursed, and neither is the man. Her pain in childbearing and his toil on the land are the same Hebrew noun, *ʿitstsavon*. The chapter never uses the word "sin," which first appears in 4.7, spoken to Cain. The fruit is never an apple. The apple came from a Latin pun on *malum*, "evil" and "apple."
The first killing · 4 | Violence arrives within a generation of the garden, and God answers it with a mark of protection, not an execution. The first city in the book is founded by the murderer. Genesis isn't sentimental about civilization.
The flood · 6–9 | The counting runs two ways at once, pairs of animals and sevens, forty days and a hundred and fifty, which source critics read as two accounts woven together. Mesopotamian flood stories share the structure and differ sharply on why it happens. This one ends with a one-sided promise never to do it again, and the sign is a war bow hung in the clouds.
The tower · 11 | One people, one language and a building project so they won't be scattered. They're scattered anyway. INTERPRETATION: Read beside an empire that standardizes speech and centralizes construction, it's the most politically pointed story in the first eleven chapters, and the one Dominion would find hardest to teach honestly.
contested: "He shall rule over you" · 3.16
Prescription or description. Read as prescription, God sets male rule in place. Read as description, the verse names what the break has done to the pair. The word for "desire," *teshuqah*, appears three times in the Hebrew Bible: here, in 4.7 where sin's desire is for Cain, and in Song 7.10, the man's desire for the woman. The Empire has never once presented this as a dispute.
/end
prompt: Which of the four is Dominion's founding myth, and which is its indictment | fill

@page rh="GENESIS · 5 OF 16 · CHAPTERS 12–50" m="GEN 12–25; GEN 24–27; GEN 29–35; GEN 37–50; GEN 41.34–36; GEN 47.13–26; EXOD 1.8–11; SPEISER, GENESIS (AB)"
# Four generations
One household carrying a promise it keeps putting at risk. Read the right column before writing anything about inheritance in the Empire.
tabrl:
| Generation | What happens | How the order breaks |
| Abraham and Sarah | Called to leave, promised land and children, and childless for decades. Sarah gives Hagar to Abraham, and Hagar bears Ishmael. Hagar flees once and is expelled once. A covenant, a near-sacrifice, and a burial cave that's the only land actually acquired. | The firstborn isn't the heir. The enslaved woman's son gets his own promise. |
| Isaac and Rebekah | The quietest generation. Rebekah asks God about the struggle in her womb, gets the oracle directly and acts on it. | The younger twin is named before birth, and the mother makes it happen. |
| Jacob, Leah and Rachel | Deception both ways, two sisters married to one man, four mothers of twelve sons, twenty years of labor and a night of wrestling that leaves him renamed and limping. | The unloved wife bears the priests and the kings. The blessing arrives through a lie and isn't withdrawn. |
| Joseph and his brothers | Sold by his brothers, jailed on a false accusation, raised to run an empire's grain and reunited with a family he can now feed or destroy. | The eleventh son governs. Judah carries the royal line. |
box: The Joseph material is an administration story
A famine is forecast and grain is centralized. Over the crisis the population sells its money, its livestock, its land and finally itself to the crown for food (47.13–26). The Hebrew says he moved the people into the cities, and the Samaritan and Greek texts say he made them slaves. The priests keep their land. A fifth of every harvest goes to Pharaoh "to this day," and the people thank him. Exodus opens on the consequence. CONTESTED: Whether Genesis presents the policy as wise government or as the start of a trap. The text praises his wisdom and records the cost without comment.
/end
prompt: Which Dominion policy is the Joseph administration, and who in the Empire admires him | fill

@page rh="GENESIS · 6 OF 16 · THE COVENANT LADDER" m="GEN 9.8–17; GEN 12.1–3; GEN 15; GEN 17; GEN 16.10–13; GEN 21.17–18; GEN 28.10–22; GEN 49.8–12; ANE SUZERAIN TREATIES"
# Seven promises
The book's spine is a promise made seven times, often to someone with less standing to receive it, each with a different sign. Note what's asked in return. For most of them, almost nothing.
tabrl:
| To whom | What's promised, and what's asked | The sign |
| Noah and every living creature · 9 | No flood will destroy the earth again. Made with all flesh, and nothing is required. The only unconditional promise in the book. | A bow in the clouds |
| Abram at the call · 12 | Land, a great nation, a great name, and blessing for "all the families of the earth." Asked only to leave. | None. Only the going. |
| Abram between the pieces · 15 | Descendants like the stars, the land, and four hundred years of oppression first. A firepot and torch pass between the halves. God alone passes through, and Abram sleeps. | Fire between the pieces |
| Abraham and Sarah renamed · 17 | Many nations, kings, an everlasting covenant, a son by Sarah. Asked: circumcision of every male in the household, slaves included. | Flesh |
| Hagar, twice in the wilderness · 16, 21 | Descendants too many to count and a great nation from Ishmael. She's enslaved, foreign and expelled, and the promise is in the patriarch's terms. | A well, and a name for God |
| Jacob at Bethel · 28 | The same promise, to a man running from the brother he cheated. Jacob answers with a conditional vow of his own. | A stone, and a stairway |
| Judah, in the deathbed blessing · 49 | His brothers will bow and the scepter won't leave him. Spoken by the dying Jacob and not by God. "Until Shiloh comes" is one of the most disputed phrases in the book. | Words only |
box: The structure underneath
INTERPRETATION: The ceremony in chapter 15 borrows the shape of an ancient treaty between a greater power and a lesser one and inverts the key term, since the greater power walks the path alone. It's a reading, and a well-supported one.
/end
prompt: Which promise Dominion claims to stand on, and which terms it leaves out to do it | fill

@page rh="GENESIS · 7 OF 16 · THE FAMILY LINE" m="GEN 4.25; GEN 21.10–12; GEN 25.23; GEN 38.27–30; GEN 48.13–20; GEN 49.3–4; 1 CHR 5.1–2; RUTH 4.18–22; TKC: TL · MEN"
# Who inherits, and why not
In every generation the promise passes over the firstborn. The Empire runs on birth order, and this is the book it says it runs on.
tabrl:
| Firstborn | Passed over for | Where |
| Cain | Seth, after Abel's death | 4.25 |
| Ishmael | Isaac. The displaced son still receives a nation. | 21.10–12 |
| Esau | Jacob, by oracle, appetite and deception. Nobody undoes it afterward. | 25.23, 27 |
| Reuben | Judah for rule, Joseph for the double portion | 49.3–4, 8–12, 22–26 |
| Manasseh | Ephraim, by Jacob's crossed hands | 48.13–20 |
| Zerah | Perez, who breaks out first at birth | 38.27–30 |
k: Women decide several of these
Sarah demands Hagar's expulsion, and God tells Abraham to listen to her (21.12). Rebekah engineers Jacob's blessing and takes the risk on herself, "Upon me be your curse, my son" (27.13). Tamar forces Judah to acknowledge her son. INTERPRETATION: Genesis treats inheritance as election and never as law. The legal heir is the one person the story reliably doesn't choose.
prompt: Where the Vane succession sits on this page, and who is the Ishmael of that house | fill

@page rh="GENESIS · 8 OF 16 · THE WOMEN" m="GEN 16; GEN 21; GEN 24–27; GEN 29–30; GEN 34; GEN 38; FRYMER-KENSKY, READING THE WOMEN OF THE BIBLE; WILLIAMS, SISTERS IN THE WILDERNESS"
# Six women, six levers
note: The Dinah row concerns sexual violence and a massacre.
Genesis pays unusual attention to women without being kind to them. Each of these six works inside a system that gives her no formal power, and each finds a different lever. The character work comes from this page.
tabrl:
| Who | What she does, and what it costs | The lever |
| Hagar | Enslaved, given to Abram as a wife, treated harshly with the verb Exodus uses for Egypt. She flees once and is expelled once. She's the only person in scripture who names God, and she does it alone. She later finds Ishmael a wife, head of her own household. | None. She has none, and is met anyway. |
| Sarah | Barren from her first mention, twice passed off as Abraham's sister to protect him, and the one who demands Hagar's expulsion. She laughs at the promise and denies it. God names the boy "he laughs," and she plays on the name afterward. | Household authority, used against a woman with less. |
| Rebekah | Answers "I will go" about her own departure, asks God directly about her pregnancy, and redirects the blessing. Her death is never narrated. | Information, and the willingness to act on it first. |
| Leah | Married by deception to a man who wanted her sister, and "hated," the text says. Names her sons as running commentary on her marriage. Mother of Levi and Judah, priests and kings. Buried beside Jacob, where Rachel isn't. | Endurance, and the naming of sons. |
| Dinah | CONTESTED: Most readers take chapter 34 as rape, and some read the verb as shaming more than force. Used as the pretext for a massacre by her brothers. She doesn't speak once. | Silence. The text's, not hers. |
| Tamar | Denied the marriage owed her, she veils herself, takes Judah's seal, cord and staff as a pledge, and produces them when he orders her burned. "She is more righteous than I." | Documentation. She keeps the receipt. |
prompt: Which of the six is Eden, which is Celeste, and which is the one nobody in TKC has been yet | fill

@page rh="GENESIS · 9 OF 16 · THE WOMEN, WORKED · WRITING" m="YOURS; WRITING; PART II, WOMEN OF THE TEXT"
# One woman, all the way down
Pick one from the facing page and work her fully. Don't pick the one you already have an opinion about.
box: The pattern worth taking
INTERPRETATION: Every one of them works the system more than escaping it, and the two who win outright, Rebekah and Tamar, win with information and evidence. POSSIBLE: Eden's method is on this page. So is Celeste's, who wrote "deceased" on the Empire's authority line and made the form answer to it (DR3).
/end
