@page rh="GENESIS · 10 OF 16 · CONTESTED IN THIS BOOK" m="GEN 1.27; GEN 3.16; GEN 9.20–27; GEN 19; EZEK 16.49; GEN 22; GOLDENBERG, THE CURSE OF HAM; LEVENSON, DEATH AND RESURRECTION OF THE BELOVED SON"
# Six fault lines
Sorted by the four kinds of disagreement from the Contested Readings module: I scholarly, II denominational, III open theological, IV unsupported.
tabrl:
| The dispute | Kind | What's actually at stake |
| The image of God | III | Whether it names a capacity, a status, a function or a relationship, and whether it's held individually or only together. The text doesn't define it, and every definition since has been an argument about who qualifies. |
| "He shall rule over you" | I | Prescription or description. The Hebrew permits both, and specialists divide. |
| The curse of Ham | IV | The text curses Canaan, not Ham, says nothing about skin, and was attached centuries later to justify racial slavery. A documented misuse more than a contested reading, and the most important thing in this book for TKC to get exactly right. |
| Sodom | II | What the city is condemned for. The narrative centers a mob's violent demand against guests under protection. Ezekiel names pride, excess and refusing the poor (16.49). Traditions differ. |
| The binding of Isaac | III | A test passed, a test failed, a polemic against child sacrifice, or a horror the text won't soften. Sarah isn't told, and in the narrative Abraham and Sarah never speak again. |
| Genre of chapters 1–11 | II | Historical report, theological narrative, or a kind of writing modern categories don't divide well. The difference drives most modern conflict over the book. |
box: The one to handle with both hands
Row three. A category IV claim built into centuries of law and preaching is exactly what Dominion would still teach, and what a novel mustn't reproduce carelessly while depicting it. Write the Empire believing it. Don't let the companion be vague about its falsity.
/end
prompt: Which of the six Dominion teaches as settled, and who in the Empire knows otherwise | fill

