@page rh="GENESIS · 13 OF 16 · GENESIS AFTER GENESIS" m="EXOD 1.8–14; EZEK 16.49; RUTH 4.18–22; MATT 1.1–17; ROM 4; ROM 5.12–21; GAL 3; GAL 4.21–31; 1 TIM 2.11–15; REV 22.1–5"
# Who used it next
Genesis is quoted, argued with and rewritten across the rest of the collection. Every later use is somebody deciding what the book meant, which is what Dominion does, with a printing press and a lectionary.
tabrl:
| Who | What they do with it |
| Exodus | Opens on the result of Joseph's administration. The descendants of the man who centralized the grain are the forced labor. INTERPRETATION: The two books are one argument, and almost nobody teaches them that way. |
| The prophets | Reach back to Abraham and Sodom as precedent. Ezekiel names Sodom's sin as pride, plenty and refusing the poor, a reading of Genesis printed inside the Bible. |
| Ruth | Closes by tracing David's line through Perez, Tamar's son. The line has always run through outsiders and irregular unions. |
| Matthew | Opens the New Testament with a Genesis-style genealogy and names Tamar in it, with Rahab, Ruth and "the wife of Uriah." |
| Paul | Uses Abraham to argue the promise came before the law, Adam as a pattern for Christ, and Hagar as an allegory (Gal 4). Genesis as precedent for a present fight, the same move Dominion makes, aimed differently. |
| The Pastorals | Cite the order of creation and the deception in the garden as grounds for restricting women's teaching (1 Tim 2.13–14). For TKC, the most consequential later use of Genesis 2–3. |
| Revelation | Ends the collection by reversing the opening: a garden inside a city, the tree open again, the serpent finished. |
k: Afterlives, briefly
Jewish midrash treats the gaps as invitations and argues in the margins for centuries. Christian allegory reads the garden forward into doctrine. The Reformation reads it for law and grace. Modern criticism asks who wrote it and when. Each tradition answers a different question, and none reads it cold.
prompt: Which later use Dominion inherited without knowing it was an interpretation | fill

@page rh="GENESIS · 14 OF 16 · WHAT WAS DONE WITH IT" m="GEN 9.20–27; GEN 2–3; 1 TIM 2.11–15; GEN 1.28; GEN 3.16; GOLDENBERG, THE CURSE OF HAM; HAYNES, NOAH'S CURSE; WHITE, “HISTORICAL ROOTS” (1967)"
# Four documented misuses
These aren't contested readings. They're historical uses of the book to justify harm, and a novel about an empire built on scripture has to know them well enough not to reproduce them by accident.
rec:
I · The so-called curse of Ham | Noah curses Canaan, Ham's son, and not Ham (9.25). Nothing in the passage mentions skin, color or Africa. David Goldenberg's *The Curse of Ham* (2003) traces how later interpreters attached blackness to Ham and slavery to blackness, and shows the supposed derivation of "Ham" from a word for "black" doesn't hold. In the Atlantic world it became a standard justification for enslaving Africans. Stephen Haynes documents its central place in American proslavery argument in *Noah's Curse* (2002).
II · Order of creation as permanent rank | Made second, so subordinate. Deceived first, so untrustworthy. Built by reading the second account as a hierarchy chart and setting the first aside, and used to bar women from teaching, testifying, holding property and holding office. INTERPRETATION: Order alone settles rank in neither account, since the animals come before both humans in chapter 1.
III · Dominion as license | A grant over the earth and its creatures turned into a warrant for extraction, conquest and taking inhabited land. The verse concerns creatures and soil and says nothing about authority over other peoples. Lynn White Jr. made the charge famous in 1967, and the stewardship reading is the main answer.
IV · Childbearing as a sentence | Pain in childbirth read as a penalty women should bear, and used to refuse them relief. When James Young Simpson began using chloroform in labor in 1847, he published an answer to religious objections. CONTESTED: Historians debate how widespread the clerical opposition really was.
box: How to write these
Let Dominion hold them sincerely and argue them well, which is what makes the Empire frightening more than cartoonish. Don't let the companion be vague about whether they're true. Sincerity and falsity are both required, and they belong on different pages.
/end
prompt: Which of the four is law in Dominion, and who teaches it believing it | fill

@page rh="GENESIS · 15 OF 16 · DOMINION'S GENESIS" m="TKC: DOM · AM; GEN 1.26–28; THE SIX INSTRUMENTS, MODULE II"
# What the Empire took
Four doctrines Dominion draws from this book, each traceable to a verse and each needing the rest of the chapter left out. Fill the right column with the instrument from the six: the calendar, the readings, clergy access, schoolbooks, provincial editions or commentary.
tab:
| Doctrine | What has to be left out for it to hold | Instrument |
| Order of the household | The first creation account entirely, and the fact that the ruling sentence sits in a description of loss more than a design specification. | _ |
| Fruitfulness as duty | That the blessing is given to humanity together, and that three of the book's matriarchs are barren for years without being judged for it. | _ |
| Dominion itself | That the grant covers the earth and its creatures and never other people, and that the Empire's name comes from a verse that doesn't authorize it. | _ |
| Order restored after collapse | That the flood story ends with a promise never to do it again, which makes divine catastrophe a poor template for a state that stages one. | _ |
corr: The old page said "four of the book's central women" were barren. Three are, Sarah (11.30), Rebekah (25.21) and Rachel (29.31). Leah is the fertile one. It also said Hagar was driven out twice. She flees once and is expelled once.
box: The name problem
The Dominions are named after a verse about rule over the earth and its creatures (AM). INFERENCE: The verse is 1.28, or 1.26, the only places in Genesis where humans are told to "have dominion." Somebody in the founding generation chose that word and knew what it said. OPEN QUESTION: Who named the Dominions, and does the Temple teach the verse as rule or as care?
/end
prompt: Who named the Dominions, and whether it was piety or a joke | fill

